  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 AM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nAscent is quickly fading away this morning as the the upper-level \nwave shifts to the east and cooler, drier air moves southward in the \nlower-levels. This will result in continued weakening of the already \nweak radar returns showing up as of 07Z. In fact, anticipate \nprecipitation to have completely wound down by 12Z. With \nincreasing subsidence, skies should clear out quickly as well, \nand this can already be seen upstream across Nebraska and Iowa \nin the Nighttime Microphysics RGB satellite imagery. At the \nsurface, that cooler and drier air is being ushered into the \nregion with a 1025mb surface high sliding south out of Canada \nand into the Plains. That high pressure will be primary \ninfluence on our weather through Saturday, leading to highs \ntoday in the mid 60s, about 10 degrees below normal, with some \nrecovery for Saturday as the high shifts into the eastern Great \nLakes. Saturday evening/night, another shortwave will track \nacross the northern Plains and Upper Midwest. This will move \nanother cold front south through the area with another push of \nslightly cooler but continued drier air south into the region. \nIt really isn't until the later half of the week, when low-level\nflow turns southerly again, that we see a rebound back to above\nnormal temperatures. Normal high temperatures for mid to late \nnext week are in the lower 70s and we'll see highs about 10 \ndegrees above normal for this time of year, based on the \navailable guidance. \n\nWith little or no moisture return into the area, as well as an \nabsence of any shortwave moving through the area, precipitation \nchances are slim to none for the next week at least. We have a dry \nforecast going right now and based on available guidance, there is \nnearly no chance for rain over the next week. The one caveat, \nat this point, would be Wednesday, when a weak front moves into\nthe area. Still, the probability of even seeing a hundredth of \nan inch is only around 5-10%.",
